
{"id":"271e9093-b3e2-45ff-9c42-879f7dfece07","object":"chat.completion","created":1784998359,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    items = case['items']\\n    required = case['required_failure_items']\\n    required_set = set(required)\\n    current = list(items)\\n    attempts = 0\\n    n = 2\\n    while True:\\n        L = len(current)\\n        if n > L:\\n            n = L\\n        removed = False\\n        for i in range(n):\\n            chunk_start = (i * L) // n\\n            chunk_end = ((i + 1) * L) // n\\n            if i == n - 1:\\n                chunk_end = L\\n            candidate = current[:chunk_start] + current[chunk_end:]\\n            attempts += 1\\n            if set(candidate).issuperset(required_set):\\n                current = candidate\\n                removed = True\\n                break\\n        if not removed:\\n            if n == L:\\n                break\\n            else:\\n                n *= 2\\n    # compute one_minimal explicitly\\n    one_minimal = True\\n    for i in range(len(current)):\\n        candidate = current[:i] + current[i+1:]\\n        if set(candidate).issuperset(required_set):\\n            one_minimal = False\\n            break\\n    return {\\n        'attempts': attempts,\\n        'one_minimal': one_minimal,\\n        'reduced_items': current\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-CR-02. The function receives a payload with \"items\" (list of strings), \"required_failure_items\" (list of strings), and possibly \"_case_nonce\". We need to perform chunk reduction following the described algorithm. The output must be a JSON object with keys: \"attempts\" (integer), \"one_minimal\" (boolean), \"reduced_items\" (list of strings).\n\nAlgorithm description: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: We have a list of items. We want to reduce it by removing chunks (contiguous subsequences) such that after removal, the set of remaining items still contains all required_failure_items. We start with granularity = 2 (i.e., chunk size? or number of chunks? Usually granularity might refer to the number of chunks to split the list into. But description says \"chunk removal at granularity two\" and \"double granularity\". Let's think: In delta debugging or similar, granularity often refers to the number of chunks. For example, granularity 2 means split into 2 chunks, try removing each chunk. If a removal is successful (required failure items still present), accept it and restart with same granularity? Actually says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\". That suggests after a successful removal, we increase granularity? \"slightly coarser\" means larger chunk size? Wait: \"coarser\" means larger chunks, i.e., fewer chunks? Typically, granularity refers to chunk size: smaller granularity means finer (more chunks). But here: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" Then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" - doubling granularity makes it coarser (larger chunks). That matches \"slightly coarser\" after accepted removal, but they say \"slightly coarser\", not double. Hmm.\n\nMaybe they mean: granularity is the number of chunks? Start with 2 chunks. Try removing each chunk (left to right). If a removal keeps required items, accept it, then restart with a \"slightly coarser\" granularity, i.e., increase number of chunks? That would be finer, not coarser. Let's read carefully: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: Granularity refers to chunk size (number of items per chunk?). At granularity two, each chunk is size 2? That would be 5 chunks for 10 items. But they say \"try chunks from left to right\" – there would be many chunks. Then after successful removal, \"slightly coarser granularity\" means larger chunk size? So increase chunk size. When no removal succeeds, double granularity (increase chunk size more). Stop at singleton granularity (chunk size = 1? That would be each item individually). That makes sense: Start with chunk size 2, then if removal succeeds, we increase chunk size a bit (maybe +1?), if no removal succeeds, we double chunk size. But \"stop at singleton granularity\" means chunk size = 1 is the finest? Actually singleton means each item alone, which is finer than size 2. So granularity decreasing? \"Singleton\" means one item per chunk, which is fine granularity. Starting at granularity two (chunk size 2) and then doubling when no removal succeeds would make chunks larger (coarser). That doesn't lead to singleton. Unless \"granularity\" means number of chunks: start with 2 chunks, double to 4, then 8, etc., until singleton (each item its own chunk). That matches typical delta debugging: split into n chunks, try each, if a subset fails, restart with that subset and same granularity? But here it's removal, not subset. The algorithm described seems akin to \"chunk reduction\" where we remove entire chunks. Let's assume granularity is the number of chunks we split the current list into. Start with 2 chunks. Try each chunk removal (i.e., remove that chunk from list). If removal keeps required items, accept and restart with a \"slightly coarser\" granularity – meaning fewer chunks? That would be 1 chunk? That seems odd. Alternatively, after successful removal, we restart with the same granularity? But they say \"slightly coarser\". Could be a misinterpretation. Let's look at the fixtures: They have items list of 10 strings t0..t9. Required failure items are two specific ones. The algorithm should reduce the list by removing items that are not required while preserving the required ones. The expected output? Not given. But we can infer from typical delta debugging: We want to minimize the list such that required items remain. This is like \"minimal failing test case\" but with removal. The algorithm described: start with granularity 2 meaning split into 2 chunks? Then try removing each chunk. If removal still contains required items, we keep that removal and restart with a \"slightly coarser\" granularity (maybe meaning we increase chunk size to 4? Actually coarser means larger chunks, so fewer chunks). That would make the algorithm progressively try larger chunks. Then when no removal succeeds, double granularity (make chunks even larger, i.e., fewer chunks). Stop at singleton granularity (each item individually). But singleton is the finest, not coarsest. So maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we go down to granularity 1 (each item). That would be the opposite direction: start coarse, then finer. Typical delta debugging starts with coarse granularity (e.g., 2 chunks) and then if removal fails, it halves the granularity (makes finer). Here it says \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\", which would make coarser, not finer. So it's inverted.\n\nLet's search memory: There is an algorithm called \"chunk reduction\" or \"ddmin\" that uses binary search: split into n chunks, try each, if removal works, restart with that subset and same n. If none works but subset of chunks together works, then increase n. But here it's about removal, not about subset.\n\nGiven the description is somewhat ambiguous, I'll derive a plausible algorithm from the text:\n\n- Start with granularity = 2 (maybe meaning chunk size = 2 items per chunk). \n- Try removing each chunk in left-to-right order. For each chunk, if after removal the required failure items are still present in the remaining items, accept that removal (i.e., permanently remove that chunk) and then restart from the beginning with a \"slightly coarser granularity\" – maybe increase chunk size by 1? Or to 3? Then continue.\n- If for a given granularity, no chunk removal is successful (i.e., removing any single chunk causes loss of required items), then double the granularity (so chunk size becomes 2*current). Stop when granularity becomes 1 (singleton). But starting at 2, doubling would give 4,8,16... never 1. So maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means when granularity becomes 1 (after doubling? if start at 2, doubling gives 4, then 8... can't reach 1). So perhaps granularity is the number of chunks, not size. Start with 2 chunks. Try removing each chunk. If success, restart with same number of chunks? Or increase? \"slightly coarser\" – maybe after success, we increase the number of chunks (more chunks = finer)? That would be \"finer\", not coarser. \"Coarser\" means fewer chunks. So maybe after success, we decrease number of chunks (coarser). That is: start with 2, after a successful removal, restart with 1 chunk? That doesn't make sense. Alternatively, after success, we restart with the same granularity but only on the reduced list? That is typical: when a removal works, we keep it and continue with same granularity on the reduced list. Then when no removal works, we increase granularity (more chunks) to try finer removals. That matches delta debugging: start with 2 chunks, if removal works, continue with same number of chunks on new smaller list; if no removal works, increase number of chunks (halve chunk size). \"Double granularity\" then would mean double the number of chunks (i.e., make finer). And \"stop at singleton granularity\" means each chunk is a single item (max number of chunks = length). That fits: start with granularity = 2 (2 chunks), after no success, double to 4, then 8, etc., until each item is its own chunk (singleton). And after a successful removal, we restart with same granularity? But the text says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That conflicts. Let's consider the wording might be reversed: \"slightly coarser\" might mean \"slightly finer\"? Or maybe they mean \"restart with slightly coarser granularity\" meaning after removal, the list is smaller, so the same number of chunks results in coarser chunks (since chunks are larger relative to smaller list). That could be: after removing a chunk, the list size decreases, so using the same number of chunks will make each chunk larger (coarser) relative to the list. That is plausible: we keep the same granularity (number of chunks), but since the list shrinks, the chunk size increases (coarser). So \"slightly coarser granularity\" could refer to the chunk size becoming larger. That interpretation: start with granularity = 2 (number of chunks). Try removing each chunk. If success, we accept removal, and now we have a smaller list, but we keep the same number of chunks (2), so each chunk is now larger (coarser). Then we try again from left to right. If no removal succeeds, we increase the number of chunks (double granularity) to make them finer. Stop when granularity (number of chunks) exceeds list length, i.e., when each chunk is a single item (singleton). That is a standard algorithm: try to remove large chunks, if not, split finer.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Let current_list = copy of items.\n- Let n = 2 (initial number of chunks).\n- While n <= len(current_list) (or until singleton? Actually when n > len, we can't split into n chunks, so we can set n = len(current_list) and treat each item as a chunk).\n- For each chunk index i from 0 to n-1 (left to right):\n    - Compute the chunk boundaries: split current_list into n chunks as evenly as possible. For n chunks, chunk size can be either floor(len/n) or ceil. Standard: make first len%n chunks have size ceil(len/n) and rest have floor. Or just split by dividing index equally. To be deterministic, use a common method: for i in range(n), chunk_i = current_list[ start_i : end_i ] where start_i = i * len // n, end_i = (i+1)*len//n? But that may miss some elements if not divisible. Better: use list slicing based on indices computed by integer division. We'll define a helper.\n    - Compute candidate = current_list with that chunk removed.\n    - Check if all required_failure_items are in candidate (as set, order doesn't matter).\n    - If yes, set current_list = candidate, and break out of the loop (accept removal). Then continue with same n (but since list is shorter, it's coarser). Note: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" suggests we restart the loop from the beginning with same n. So after acceptance, we restart the while loop (but n unchanged). So we need to restart the inner loop from i=0.\n- If no chunk removal succeeded for all chunks (i.e., loop completes without break), then double n: n = n * 2. Then continue the while loop (but note: if n becomes > len(current_list), we can set n = len(current_list) (or stop). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" That means when n exceeds len, we set n = len to make each chunk singleton. Then try again (since now chunks are singletons). If still no removal (i.e., no single item can be removed because all are required), then stop.\n- Also, we need to track attempts: each check of a chunk removal counts as an attempt? The output \"attempts\" likely counts how many removals were tried (i.e., number of candidate checks). So increment attempts each time we test a removal.\n\n- At the end, after no more removals possible, we have reduced_items = current_list.\n- \"one_minimal\" check: A set of items is one-minimal if removing any single item (singleton) changes the property (i.e., required failure items are no longer present). But the algorithm should stop when no singleton removal succeeds. However, the algorithm may stop earlier if n exceeds length and we set n=len, then try singleton removals. But if any singleton removal succeeds, we would have accepted it and restarted. So after exiting the loop, no singleton removal can succeed. Thus one_minimal should be True if we have performed the singleton check. But need to ensure that the algorithm indeed reaches singleton check. The description says \"stop at singleton granularity\", meaning we will eventually try singleton chunks. So after loop, one_minimal should be True. But perhaps there is a nuance: The algorithm might stop early if n becomes too large? We'll implement exactly: start n=2, while n <= len(current_list): try removals; if no success, n *= 2; if n > len(current_list): set n = len(current_list) (singleton) and do one more iteration; after that, if still no success, exit. Then one_minimal = True because we have checked all singletons and none could be removed. However, if the loop exits because len(current_list) == 0? Not possible since required_failure_items must remain, so at least those items remain.\n\nEdge cases: initial items list may be empty? But fixtures have items.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- def solve(case):\n    items = case['items']\n    required = case['required_failure_items']\n    required_set = set(required)\n    current = list(items)\n    attempts = 0\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        L = len(current)\n        if n > L:\n            # stop at singleton: set n = L\n            n = L\n        # Try chunks from left to right\n        removed = False\n        for i in range(n):\n            # compute chunk indices\n            chunk_start = (i * L) // n\n            chunk_end = ((i+1) * L) // n\n            # Ensure last chunk includes any remainder\n            if i == n-1:\n                chunk_end = L\n            # Build candidate list without that chunk\n            candidate = current[:chunk_start] + current[chunk_end:]\n            attempts += 1\n            # Check if required items still present\n            if set(candidate).issuperset(required_set):\n                current = candidate\n                removed = True\n                break  # restart with same n (loop will restart because we break inner loop but need to restart while loop)\n        if not removed:\n            if n == L:  # already singleton and no removal, done\n                break\n            else:\n                n *= 2\n                # if doubled n exceeds L, next iteration will set n=L\n        # else, removed: we continue with same n (but due to shorter L, chunks become coarser)\n        # The outer while loop continues.\n    reduced_items = current\n    one_minimal = True  # because we stopped at singleton and no removal succeeded\n    # But double-check: Could there be a scenario where we exit with n < L and never tried singletons? For example, if n doubles beyond L, we set n=L and try singletons, then if no removal, break. So one_minimal should be true.\n    # However, if L becomes 1 and required item is that one, n=1, we try chunk removal (the whole list) which would remove all items, failing because required not present. So no removal, break. one_minimal true.\n    return {\"attempts\": attempts, \"one_minimal\": one_minimal, \"reduced_items\": reduced_items}\n\nBut we need to be careful about the exact chunk division. The algorithm says \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" It doesn't specify how chunks are defined. Using integer division is common. Also, after acceptance, \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" In our implementation, after removal we don't change n, but because list is shorter, the same n yields coarser chunks (larger chunk sizes). That matches.\n\nLet's test mentally with fixture a-000: items t0..t9, required t0 and t9.\nStart n=2, L=10. Chunks: [t0..t4] and [t5..t9] (since 10/2=5). Try removing first chunk: candidate = t5..t9. Does it contain t0? No, t0 missing. So not accepted. Try removing second chunk: candidate = t0..t4. Contains t0 but also t9? No, t9 missing. So no removal. Then n doubles to 4. L=10, n=4. Chunks: first 2 items (10//4=2, remainder 2) so chunks: indices: [0:2] = t0,t1; [2:4]=t2,t3; [4:6]=t4,t5; [6:10]=t6,t7,t8,t9? Actually better to compute: (0*10)//4=0, (1*10)//4=2, (2*10)//4=5, (3*10)//4=7, (4*10)//4=10. Wait that gives chunks: 0-2, 2-5,5-7,7-10. That's [t0,t1], [t2,t3,t4], [t5,t6], [t7,t8,t9]? Not consistent. We'll implement as described: chunk_start = (i*L)//n, chunk_end = ((i+1)*L)//n, and for last chunk ensure end = L. For L=10, n=4: (0*10)//4=0, (1*10)//4=2, (2*10)//4=5, (3*10)//4=7, (4*10)//4=10. So chunks: [0:2]=t0,t1; [2:5]=t2,t3,t4; [5:7]=t5,t6; [7:10]=t7,t8,t9. Try removing each. Remove first: candidate = t2..t9 -> has t0? No, missing t0. Remove second: candidate = t0,t1,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9 -> has t0 and t9? t0 present, t9 present -> accept! So current becomes that list (7 items). attempts = 4 (chunks checked: 2 from n=2, then 2 from n=4? Actually attempts count each check. We'll increment each iteration. For n=2: 2 attempts. Then n=4: attempt 3 (first chunk of n=4), attempt 4 (second chunk) and accept. So total attempts=4. Then restart with n still 4? But now L=7. n=4 > L? 4 <= 7, so continue. Chunks: L=7, n=4: (0*7)//4=0, (1*7)//4=1, (2*7)//4=3, (3*7)//4=5, (4*7)//4=7 => chunks: [0:1]=t0; [1:3]=t1,t2? Actually candidate current = [t0,t1,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9]. Indices: 0:t0,1:t1,2:t5,3:t6,4:t7,5:t8,6:t9. So chunks: [0:1]=[t0]; [1:3]=[t1,t5]; [3:5]=[t6,t7]; [5:7]=[t8,t9]. Try remove first: candidate without t0 => [t1,t5,t6,t7,t8,t9] -> missing t0, reject. Remove second: candidate without [t1,t5] => [t0,t6,t7,t8,t9] -> contains t0 and t9? Yes, t0 present, t9 present -> accept. So current becomes [t0,t6,t7,t8,t9] (5 items). attempts now 6. L=5, n=4 still? 4<=5. Chunks: L=5,n=4: (0*5)//4=0, (1*5)//4=1, (2*5)//4=2, (3*5)//4=3, (4*5)//4=5 => chunks: [0:1]=[t0]; [1:2]=[t6]; [2:3]=[t7]; [3:5]=[t8,t9]. Try remove first: missing t0, reject. Remove second: candidate = [t0,t7,t8,t9] -> contains t0 and t9? t0 present, t9 present -> accept. current = [t0,t7,t8,t9] (4 items). attempts=8. L=4,n=4. Chunks: L=4,n=4: each item a chunk: [0:1]=t0; [1:2]=t7; [2:3]=t8; [3:4]=t9. Try remove t0: missing t0 -> reject. t7: candidate [t0,t8,t9] -> contains t0 and t9? t0 present, t9 present -> accept. current = [t0,t8,t9] (3). attempts=9. L=3,n=4 => n > L? n=4 > 3, so set n=L=3. Now L=3,n=3. Chunks: each item. Try remove t0: reject (missing t0). t8: candidate [t0,t9] -> contains t0 and t9 -> accept. current = [t0,t9] (2). attempts=10. L=2,n=3 => n>2, set n=2. Chunks: [t0] and [t9]. Try remove t0: reject; t9: reject. No removal. n=2 == L, so break. Final reduced_items = [t0,t9]. attempts = 10. one_minimal = True.\n\nThus algorithm yields minimal set [t0,t9]. For other fixtures, similar.\n\nEdge case: What if after removal, L becomes 1 and that single item is required? Then with n=1 (since n set to L), there is one chunk (the whole list). Removing it would leave empty, which fails required check. So no removal, break. That's fine.\n\nNow, we need to ensure we don't miss any possibility. The algorithm as described is deterministic.\n\nImplementation details: Use while True loop with break conditions. Also, when n doubles and exceeds L, we set n = L and continue one more iteration. The \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we will try when each chunk is a single item. So after setting n=L, we try. If no removal, break.\n\nNow, about \"one_minimal\": We need to output boolean. According to the algorithm, we stop at singleton, so it should be True. However, there is a possibility that the algorithm never reaches singleton because, for example, after doubling, n becomes > L and we set n=L, then try, if removal happens, we would have accepted and restarted with same n=L (now L smaller). That still involved singleton trials. Eventually, when L doesn't change after singleton trials, we break. So one_minimal should be True.\n\nBut let's verify: The description \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So we need to compute one_minimal. Could it be that we need to explicitly check if removing any single item from reduced_items still contains required items? That is, after algorithm, run a separate check: for each item in reduced_items, test if removing it preserves required items. If none can be removed, then one_minimal = True. If there is any such item, then one_minimal = False, but our algorithm would have removed it already if it were a singleton chunk. However, our algorithm might not have tested all singletons if it stopped earlier due to n not reaching L. But we designed to eventually set n=L. So it should be covered. But to be safe, we can explicitly compute one_minimal by testing each single removal from final reduced_items. That is straightforward: after algorithm, for each item in reduced_items, check if set(reduced_items) - {item} still contains required_set. If any can be removed, set one_minimal = False. This also serves as a verification. But the algorithm should already guarantee it, but we can still compute to be explicit.\n\nNow, output format: JSON object with keys attempts (int), one_minimal (bool), reduced_items (list of strings). Must match schema.\n\nWe need to implement solve(case) in Python.\n\nAlso, note the problem statement mentions \"registered token trees\" and \"compiler execution\" but that's context; we only have items and required_failure_items. So we just use those.\n\nNow, write the code. Ensure it's standard library only, no external imports.\n\nImportant: The case payload may have _case_nonce, but we ignore it.\n\nNow, produce the final answer as a JSON object with single key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source as a JSON string.\n\nWe must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. I'll write the code in a multi-line string and then JSON-encode it.\n\nI'll write:\n\nimport json\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['items']\n    required = case['required_failure_items']\n    required_set = set(required)\n    current = list(items)\n    attempts = 0\n    n = 2\n    while True:\n        L = len(current)\n        if n > L:\n            n = L  # stop at singleton granularity\n        removed = False\n        for i in range(n):\n            # compute chunk boundaries\n            chunk_start = (i * L) // n\n            chunk_end = ((i + 1) * L) // n\n            if i == n - 1:\n                chunk_end = L\n            candidate = current[:chunk_start] + current[chunk_end:]\n            attempts += 1\n            if set(candidate).issuperset(required_set):\n                current = candidate\n                removed = True\n                break\n        if not removed:\n            if n == L:  # already singleton and no removal\n                break\n            else:\n                n *= 2\n    # After algorithm, compute one_minimal explicitly\n    one_minimal = True\n    for i, item in enumerate(current):\n        candidate = current[:i] + current[i+1:]\n        if set(candidate).issuperset(required_set):\n            one_minimal = False\n            break\n    return {\n        \"attempts\": attempts,\n        \"one_minimal\": one_minimal,\n        \"reduced_items\": current\n    }\n\nBut note: The spec says \"must not mutate case\". We are not mutating case.\n\nNow, we need to ensure that the function returns JSON-serializable object. It does.\n\nNow, produce the JSON with the implementation string.\n\nI'll do:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"import json\\n\\ndef solve(case):\\n    items = case['items']\\n    required = case['required_failure_items']\\n    required_set = set(required)\\n    current = list(items)\\n    attempts = 0\\n    n = 2\\n    while True:\\n        L = len(current)\\n        if n > L:\\n            n = L\\n        removed = False\\n  